(window.LADDER_CARDS["13a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13a1.1","q":"What is a point of the graph of f, what is the chord through s and t, and what is the chord's slope?","a":"a point is one of f's pairs (w, f(w)); the chord is the linear function through (s, f(s)) and (t, f(t)); its slope is (f(t) − f(s))/(t − s)","w":["a point is an input of f; the chord is f between s and t; its slope is f(t) − f(s)","a point is a dot of geometry; the chord is a tangent line; its slope is (t − s)/(f(t) − f(s))","a point is an output; the chord is the interval [s, t]; its slope is f(t)/t"],"why":"No shape is meant. “At a point” means at that pair's input, and a chord needs two different inputs."},
 {"u":"13a2.1","q":"What does Δ before a name make, what is h, and why is h never 0?","a":"the change of that quantity, final less starting; h is the change of the input, x − w; a chord needs two different inputs, and the chord slope divides by h","w":["the size of the quantity; h is the output; h is never 0 because outputs are positive","the slope of the quantity; h is a tiny number; h is never 0 because it is endlessly small","the limit of the quantity; h is the height; h may be 0 at the end"],"why":"Δf = f(w + h) − f(w). The chord slope Δf/h has a hole at h = 0, and a limit never looks at the hole."},
 {"u":"13a3.1","q":"What is the slope of f at w, and what does two-ways-to-write-a-limit say?","a":"f′(w) is the limit of the chord slopes (f(w + h) − f(w))/h as h → 0, when it exists; lim_{h→0} F(w + h) = L exactly when lim_{x→w} F(x) = L","w":["f′(w) is the chord slope at h = 0; a limit can be written only one way","f′(w) is f(w)/w; the two limits differ by w","f′(w) is the slope of some chord near w; h → 0 and x → 0 are the same"],"why":"The definition is a limit, so the value at h = 0 plays no part. Writing x for w + h gives the form (f(x) − f(w))/(x − w)."},
 {"u":"13a4.1","q":"What does slope-of-a-line say, and why do the two jobs of “slope” agree for a linear function?","a":"x ↦ mx + b has slope m at every real w; every chord slope is mh/h = m, so the limit is m, the line's own slope","w":["it has slope b at every real; the chords pass through b","it has slope m only at 0; elsewhere the chords bend","it has slope mw + b; the slope is the value"],"why":"9e's slope of a line and 13a's slope at a point give one number. A constant has slope 0 and x ↦ x has slope 1."},
 {"u":"13a5.1","q":"In x²'s chord slope at 3, how does the h · h piece leave, and why may h = 0 not go into the first line?","a":"(6h + h²)/h = 6 + h for h ≠ 0, and the h goes to 0 in the limit, not by being dropped as small; with h = 0 the first line is 0/0, which names nothing","w":["h² is struck out because it is tiny; h = 0 gives 6","h² cancels against the 9; h = 0 is allowed after simplifying","h² is negative and leaves by subtraction; h = 0 gives 9"],"why":"Every line is an exact equation for h ≠ 0. Simplify first, then take the limit: (x²)′ = 2x."},
 {"u":"13a6.1","q":"What do slope-of-x³ and slope-of-one-over-x say, and why is nothing asked of 1/x at 0?","a":"(x³)′ = 3x² at every real, and (1/x)′ = −1/x² at every w ≠ 0; 0 is not an input of 1/x, and the definition starts with an input","w":["(x³)′ = x² and (1/x)′ = 1/x²; at 0 the slope is 0","(x³)′ = 3x and (1/x)′ = −1/x; at 0 the slope passes every bound","(x³)′ = 3x² and (1/x)′ = −x; at 0 the limit is 1"],"why":"The cube spreads to w³ + 3w²h + 3wh² + h³, and the pieces with h left over go to 0. A slope is asked only where the function has a value."},
 {"u":"13a7.1","q":"What is the tangent line of f at w, and what does the definition not ask of it?","a":"the linear function through (w, f(w)) with slope f′(w), x ↦ f(w) + f′(w)(x − w); it is not asked to meet the graph only once, or to stay on one side of it","w":["the chord through w and w + 1; it is not asked to pass through the point","any line that touches the graph once; it is not asked to have a slope","the line x ↦ f′(w)x; it is not asked to be linear"],"why":"x³ at 1 has the tangent line 3x − 2, which meets x³ again at −2. The point and the slope there are all that define it."},
 {"u":"13a8.1","q":"Why can a “bit” dx be no real at all, and what does the ladder do instead of striking out the h · h piece?","a":"if dx is a real other than 0, so is (dx)², and striking it out is a false equation; if dx is 0, the division is forbidden; there is no third kind of real. The ladder keeps h a real, never 0, and lets h go to 0 in the limit","w":["dx is a very small real, and the ladder rounds it off","dx is 0, and the ladder divides by it once only","dx is negative, and the ladder squares it away"],"why":"No positive real is below every 1/n. So nothing is struck out: the h · h piece, divided by h, is h, and its limit is 0."},
 {"u":"13a9.1","q":"Why are putting h = 0 into the chord slope, and “6h + h² = 6h, since h is tiny”, moves to watch out for?","a":"h = 0 makes the chord slope 0/0, which names nothing; and 6h + h² = 6h is false for every h ≠ 0, since h · h is a real other than 0","w":["h = 0 gives the wrong sign; and tiny reals are equal to 0","h = 0 is allowed for polynomials only; and h² is negative","both are sound once the limit has been taken"],"why":"Simplify for h ≠ 0, then take the limit. The h piece goes to 0 only after the division, in the limit."},
 {"u":"13a10.1","q":"Handed a limit shaped like a chord slope, how does the work open, and how does it close?","a":"read the definition right to left: find the function and the input whose chord slope it is, then give the slope by the technique already proved for that function","w":["multiply out the top and put h = 0","test h = 0.001 and report the result","draw the chord and measure it"],"why":"lim ((2 + h)³ − 8)/h is the slope of x³ at 2, since 8 = 2³: it is 12, by slope-of-x³."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13b1.1","q":"What does slope-means-no-gap say, what is the one trick of its proof, and what does change-heads-to-0 say?","a":"a slope at w makes f continuous at w; write the rise as the chord slope times the run, Δf = (Δf/h) · h; and with a slope at w, Δf heads to 0 as h → 0","w":["continuity at w gives a slope at w; divide by h twice; Δf heads to f′(w)","a slope at w makes f constant near w; put h = 0; Δf is 0 for every h","a slope at w makes f bounded on ℝ; add and take away f(w); Δf heads to h"],"why":"The chord slope heads to f′(w) and the run heads to 0. A product of something that stays put with something heading to 0 heads to 0."},
 {"u":"13b2.1","q":"What does slope-bounds-change say, and what recipe does it hand back for 11a's game?","a":"with a slope at w there is a reach r₁ and C = |f′(w)| + 1 with |f(x) − f(w)| ≤ C|x − w| for x within r₁ of w; answer a challenge ε with δ = min(r₁, ε/C)","w":["|f(x) − f(w)| ≤ |x − w| for every x; answer with δ = ε","|f(x) − f(w)| = f′(w) near w; answer with δ = 1","the change is at least C|x − w|; no δ can be given"],"why":"Near w the chord slopes are within 1 of f′(w). So a slope is more than continuity: the change is at most a fixed multiple of the distance."},
 {"u":"13b3.1","q":"What does slope-means-no-gap say turned about, when is it used, and what do chord slopes do at a discontinuity?","a":"if f is defined near w and not continuous at w, then f has no slope at w; use it to stop before computing; at a jump the chords from one side stay level while those from the other pass every bound","w":["if f has no slope at w it is not continuous at w; use it to find jumps; the chord slopes head to 0","if f is continuous it has a slope; use it at every input; the chord slopes settle on the jump's height","a discontinuity has slope 0; use it for step functions; the chords are all level"],"why":"For the step u at 0, right-hand chords have slope 0 and left-hand chords −1/h. There is nothing for them to head to."},
 {"u":"13b4.1","q":"Why is “continuous at w, so a slope at w” a move to watch out for, and which function refutes it?","a":"it affirms the consequent of slope-means-no-gap; x · q(x) is continuous at 0, and its chord slopes at 0 are q(h), which is 0 or 1 as near 0 as you like and has no limit","w":["it denies the antecedent; x² refutes it at 0","it is sound for every function; nothing refutes it","it turns the technique about; 1/x refutes it at 0"],"why":"The technique runs one way only. |x| at 0 is a second refutation, on 13f."},
 {"u":"13b5.1","q":"What does slope-at-0-only say, and what does it show about a slope at one input?","a":"x² · q(x) has slope 0 at 0, since its chord slopes h · q(h) are squeezed to 0; it is continuous at no other input, so a slope at w is a fact about w alone","w":["x² · q(x) has slope 0 everywhere; a slope spreads to a neighbourhood","x · q(x) has slope 1 at 0; a slope at w needs slopes nearby","x² · q(x) has no slope at 0; one slope implies continuity near w"],"why":"|h · q(h)| ≤ |h|. A slope at one input says nothing about the others, as continuity at a point did not."},
 {"u":"13b6.1","q":"Why is “u's chords from the right are level, so u′(0) = 0” a move to watch out for?","a":"a slope is a limit from both sides: the chords from the left have slope −1/h, which passes every bound; and u has a discontinuity at 0, so it has no slope there","w":["the right-hand chords are not level","u′(0) = 1, the height of the jump","a slope is taken from the left only"],"why":"One-sided agreement is not a limit. Slope-means-no-gap turned about settles it before any chord is computed."},
 {"u":"13b7.1","q":"Handed a function and an input and asked for a slope, how does the work open, and how does it close?","a":"look first for a discontinuity at the input: if there is one there is no slope, and you are done; if there is none the question is open, and only the chord slopes can settle it","w":["compute chord slopes at once; continuity is checked last","check continuity: if it holds, the slope exists","find the value at the input: the slope is the value"],"why":"No gap is needed for a slope and is not enough for one. Handed a slope, you have continuity at that input for free."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13c1.1","q":"What are the four cells of the new product f(w + h)g(w + h), and which make the change Δ(fg)?","a":"f(w)g(w), Δf · g(w), f(w) · Δg and Δf · Δg; the change is the three cells other than the old product f(w)g(w)","w":["f(w), g(w), Δf and Δg; the change is Δf + Δg","f(w)g(w) and Δf · Δg only; the change is Δf · Δg","four copies of f(w)g(w); the change is 0"],"why":"The new product is a product of two sums, multiplied out by the grid of cells. The boxes only keep the books: a change can be negative."},
 {"u":"13c2.1","q":"What does product for slopes say, and where is “a slope means no gap” spent in its proof?","a":"(fg)′(w) = f′(w)g(w) + f(w)g′(w); it is spent on the leftover cell, where (Δf/h) · Δg heads to f′(w) · 0 because Δg heads to 0","w":["(fg)′(w) = f′(w)g′(w); it is spent on the old product","(fg)′(w) = f′(w) + g′(w); it is not spent at all","(fg)′(w) = f(w)g(w); it is spent on dividing by h"],"why":"Each function times the slope of the other, added. Change-heads-to-0 for g is what makes the corner cell vanish in the limit."},
 {"u":"13c3.1","q":"What is the trick of the second road to the product rule, and where does it spend continuity?","a":"add and take away the same product f(w + h)g(w), so the change splits into f(w + h) · Δg + g(w) · Δf; continuity is spent in the line that sends f(w + h) to f(w)","w":["divide both factors by h; continuity is spent on g","multiply the two slopes; continuity is not needed","expand in powers of h; continuity is spent on h²"],"why":"Adding and taking away the same real changes nothing. The two roads spend the same hypotheses."},
 {"u":"13c4.1","q":"Which of 13a's slopes does product for slopes recover, and what does it give when one factor is a constant c?","a":"x · x gives 2w and x · x² gives 3w², and x · (1/x) = 1 gives 0, all agreeing with 13a; for a constant c, (cf)′ = c f′, since the constant's slope is 0","w":["it recovers only the slope of a line; (cf)′ = c + f′","it recovers none of them; (cf)′ = 0","it gives x · x the slope 1; (cf)′ = c′f′"],"why":"1 · w + w · 1 = 2w. With a constant factor the term c′f is 0 and c f′ is left."},
 {"u":"13c5.1","q":"In terms of the cells, why does the tempting rule (fg)′ = f′g′ fail?","a":"f′g′ is the limit of the leftover cell Δf · Δg divided by h twice; the change is divided by h once, and then the leftover goes to 0 while the two side cells stay","w":["the cells do not add up for small h","f′g′ counts the old product twice","the side cells are negative"],"why":"The tempting rule keeps the only piece that vanishes and throws away both pieces that stay. x · x at 3 gives 1, and the slope is 6."},
 {"u":"13c6.1","q":"Why is “drop Δf Δg because it is small” a move to watch out for?","a":"for every h ≠ 0 the four cells add up exactly, so dropping one makes a false equation; the leftover goes to 0 only after dividing by h, in the limit, by change-heads-to-0","w":["Δf Δg is the largest of the four cells","Δf Δg is negative and must be added back twice","small cells may be dropped only when h is a fraction"],"why":"Smallness is not a rule of arithmetic. The limit, not the eye, removes the corner cell."},
 {"u":"13c7.1","q":"Handed a product-rule answer such as 3x²(2x + 5) + x³ · 2, how does the work open, and how does it close?","a":"look for two factors whose slopes appear beside the other factor: 3x² is the slope of x³ and 2 the slope of 2x + 5, so the product was x³(2x + 5); check by one chord slope with a small h","w":["multiply everything out and divide by x","set the answer to 0 and solve","take the slope of the answer again"],"why":"Each term is one factor plain and the other's slope. A numeric chord slope is a cheap check on the reading."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13d1.1","q":"What does power for slopes say, and why does its proof reach whole powers only?","a":"the slope of xⁿ is n · xⁿ⁻¹; the proof climbs by induction, each next power being the one before times x with the product rule, and that climb only visits whole numbers","w":["the slope of xⁿ is xⁿ⁻¹; the proof divides by n","the slope of xⁿ is n · xⁿ; the proof works for every real power","the slope of xⁿ is (n − 1)xⁿ; the proof needs n to be even"],"why":"x to the next power is a product, and 13c has a rule for products. Fraction powers wait for their own proof."},
 {"u":"13d2.1","q":"What do constant-has-slope-0, slopes-add and constant-factor say, and what is lifting?","a":"a constant has slope 0 at every real; (f ± g)′ = f′ ± g′; (cf)′ = c f′; lifting is adding a constant to a function, which changes no slope","w":["a constant has slope c; slopes multiply; (cf)′ = c + f′; lifting doubles the slope","a constant has no slope; (f + g)′ = f′g′; (cf)′ = f′; lifting moves the input","a constant has slope 1; slopes add only for polynomials; lifting adds c to the slope"],"why":"Each is a limit technique of 10c read on chord slopes. The constant cancels in every rise, so the chords keep their slopes."},
 {"u":"13d3.1","q":"What does polynomial-slope say, and what kind of function is the slope function of a polynomial?","a":"every polynomial has a slope at every real, found term by term: p′(x) = u₁ + 2u₂x + ⋯ + n u_n xⁿ⁻¹; it is a polynomial again, of degree one less","w":["a polynomial has a slope only at its zeros; the slope function is a line","the slope is found by dividing each term by x; it is a quotient","every polynomial has the same slope function; it is a constant"],"why":"Power, constant-factor and slopes-add do it, by induction on the degree. The constant term goes to 0."},
 {"u":"13d4.1","q":"What is f″, and what is the second slope of a linear function?","a":"f″ is (f′)′, the slope of the slope; for x ↦ mx + b it is 0 at every real, because its slope is the constant m and a constant has slope 0","w":["f″ is f′ squared; for a line it is m²","f″ is the slope at two inputs; for a line it is 2m","f″ is the chord slope; for a line it is b"],"why":"It is computed by the same rules, applied to the slope function. A line's steepness never changes."},
 {"u":"13d5.1","q":"What does displaced-slopes say, why does lifting change no slope, and which of 9e's two moves do slopes follow?","a":"for g(x) = f(x − s), g′(w) = f′(w − s); in lifting the constant cancels in every rise; slopes follow the displacing of the input and ignore the lift","w":["g′(w) = f′(w) − s; lifting adds k to every slope; slopes follow the lift","g′(w) = f′(w + s); lifting halves the slopes; slopes follow neither","g′(w) = s · f′(w); lifting moves the chords sideways; slopes follow both"],"why":"The displaced graph has the same chords, moved along by s. So the slope is taken where the original input was."},
 {"u":"13d6.1","q":"Why is “(x^{1/2})′ = ½x^{−1/2}, by power for slopes” a move to watch out for?","a":"power for slopes is proved for whole powers only; ½ is not a whole number, so the technique cited does not reach it","w":["the answer should be ½x^{1/2}","roots have no slopes","power for slopes holds only for odd powers"],"why":"A right answer with the wrong citation is not a proof. Fraction powers get their own technique later on the rung."},
 {"u":"13d7.1","q":"Handed a slope function such as 3x² + 4x − 1, how does the work open, and how does it close?","a":"raise each power by one and divide by the new power, giving x³ + 2x² − x; then say that adding any constant gives another with the same slopes, and check by polynomial-slope","w":["lower each power by one and multiply by the old power","divide the slope function by x","put x = 0 and read off the constant"],"why":"It is polynomial-slope read backwards. Whether the lifts are all the answers is rung 14's question."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13e1.1","q":"In a chain such as (2x + 1)³, what are the inside and the outside, where is the outside's rate taken, and what is almost right about the obvious proof?","a":"the inside is r = 2x + 1 and the outside is r³; the outside's rate is taken where the inside stands, at r₀; the obvious proof writes Δy/h as (Δy/Δr) · (Δr/h), which is right wherever Δr ≠ 0","w":["the inside is the cube and the outside is 2x + 1; the rate is taken at x; the obvious proof adds the two rates","the inside is x and the outside is 3; the rate is taken at 0; the obvious proof is right as it stands","the inside is 2 and the outside is x³; the rate is taken at h; the obvious proof squares Δr"],"why":"Two rates in a row should multiply: 2 for the inside, 27 for the outside at 3, so 54. Multiplying and dividing by Δr is the idea, with one flaw."},
 {"u":"13e2.1","q":"Where does the obvious proof of the chain rule break, and which insides show it?","a":"it divides by Δr, which is licensed only if Δr ≠ 0 for every small h ≠ 0; a constant inside has Δr = 0 at every h, and x² · q(x) at 0 has Δr = 0 at endlessly many h near 0","w":["it divides by h, which may be 0; x² shows it","it takes a limit of a product; no inside shows it","it uses the outside's slope at the wrong place; 2x + 1 shows it"],"why":"The rule is fine and the proof needs mending. Nothing promises that the inside moves when the input does."},
 {"u":"13e3.1","q":"What is the helper w, and what does the helper technique say of it?","a":"w(k) is the outside's chord slope (f(r₀ + k) − f(r₀))/k for k ≠ 0, and w(0) = f′(r₀); then w is continuous at 0, and f(r₀ + k) − f(r₀) = w(k) · k for every small k, 0 included","w":["w(k) is the inside's change; it is 0 at every k","w(k) is f′(r₀ + k); it is constant","w(k) is k/h; it has a hole at 0 that cannot be mended"],"why":"The value chosen at 0 mends the hole, as 11f's mending chose a value. The product form holds at k = 0 too, as 0 = w(0) · 0."},
 {"u":"13e4.1","q":"What does chain for slopes say, and what does each hypothesis pay for in the proof?","a":"(f ∘ g)′(p) = f′(g(p)) · g′(p); g's slope pays twice, for Δr/h heading to g′(p) and for Δr heading to 0; f's slope pays once, for the helper's continuity at 0","w":["(f ∘ g)′(p) = f′(p) · g′(p); f's slope pays for both limits","(f ∘ g)′(p) = f′(g′(p)); g's slope is not used","(f ∘ g)′(p) = f′(g(p)) + g′(p); each slope pays for one sum"],"why":"Rates through a chain multiply, the outside's taken where the inside stands. In the mended proof Δr appears as a factor, never as a bottom."},
 {"u":"13e5.1","q":"What are the three moves of the chain rule in use, and what does slope-of-√ say and multiply by?","a":"name the inside r and find where it stands; take the outside's slope there as though r were the input; multiply by the inside's slope. √ has slope 1/(2√y) at every y > 0, proved by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate √(y + h) + √y","w":["take the slope of each piece at x and add; √ has slope ½√y, proved by squaring both sides","multiply the pieces, then take one slope; √ has slope 1/√y, proved by power for slopes","name the outside x; divide by the inside; √ has slope 2√y, proved by the product rule"],"why":"The conjugate turns the top into (y + h) − y = h. This road reaches the power ½, which 13d's induction could not."},
 {"u":"13e6.1","q":"What does dy/dx name, what is it not, and why is the chain rule in d-marks not a cancelling of dr?","a":"it names f′ when y = f(x): one mark, written as a fraction for history's sake, not a quotient of two numbers; dy/dx = dy/dr · dr/dx is chain for slopes, proved with the helper, and there is no number dr to cancel","w":["it names Δy/Δx for a small Δx; the dr's cancel as in any fraction","it names the chord slope; dr is 0, so it cancels","it names y divided by x; the chain rule is fractions-of-rationals"],"why":"The d-mark makes the rule look like arithmetic. The look is a reminder, and the proof is the technique."},
 {"u":"13e7.1","q":"What does undoing's-slope say, what proves it, and how does it refute a slope for ∛ at 0?","a":"if g undoes f near x₀ and both have slopes, g′(y₀) · f′(x₀) = 1; chain for slopes on g ∘ f, which agrees with x ↦ x, slope 1; x³ has slope 0 at 0, and nothing times 0 is 1, so ∛ has no slope at 0","w":["g′(y₀) = f′(x₀); the product rule; ∛ has slope 0 at 0","g′(y₀) + f′(x₀) = 0; slopes-add; ∛ has slope −1 at 0","g′ = 1/f; the quotient rule; ∛ has no value at 0"],"why":"The technique takes g's slope as a hypothesis. Turned about, a slope of 0 for f forbids one for its undoing there."},
 {"u":"13e8.1","q":"Why is “(f ∘ g)′(p) = f′(p) g′(p)” a move to watch out for?","a":"it takes the outside's slope at the wrong place: at p, where the input is, not at g(p), where the inside stands","w":["the two slopes should be added","the inside's slope should be taken at g(p)","it is right whenever f is a power"],"why":"(2x + 1)³ at 1: 3 · 1² · 2 = 6, but the slope is 54, with f′ taken at r₀ = 3."},
 {"u":"13e9.1","q":"Handed a chain-rule answer such as 3(x² − 4)² · 2x, how does the work open, and how does it close?","a":"read it as an outside's slope at r times an inside's slope: 2x belongs to r = x² − 4 and 3r² is the slope of r³, so the chain was (x² − 4)³; check by taking the chain apart and multiplying the two rates","w":["multiply it out and take its slope","divide it by 2x and stop","set r = 2x and cube it"],"why":"The inside's slope sits as a factor, and the outside's slope still shows the inside. Found upward, written downward."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13f1.1","q":"What does size-has-no-slope-at-0 say, and what does |x| show about the converse of slope-means-no-gap?","a":"|x| is continuous at 0 and has no slope there: its chord slopes from 0 are 1 for h > 0 and −1 for h < 0, with no one limit; so continuity does not give a slope","w":["|x| has slope 0 at 0, the average of 1 and −1; the converse holds","|x| is not continuous at 0; the converse is not tested","|x| has slope 1 at 0; the converse holds for sizes"],"why":"The two sides arrive with different slopes. Slope-means-no-gap does not run backwards."},
 {"u":"13f2.1","q":"What are the slope from the right and the slope from the left, and what does two-side-slopes say?","a":"the one-sided limits of the chord slopes as h → 0⁺ and as h → 0⁻; a function has a slope exactly when its slopes from the two sides exist and agree","w":["the slopes at w + 1 and w − 1; a slope is their average","the greatest and least chord slopes; a slope exists when either exists","the slopes of the two nearest chords; they always agree"],"why":"It is 10's two-sided limit read on chord slopes. The one-sided slopes exist even where the slope does not."},
 {"u":"13f3.1","q":"What does joint-slopes say, and what does a joint cost in continuity and in slope?","a":"at a joint, where the pieces arrive at one height, the function has a slope exactly when the pieces arrive with the same slope; a joint costs nothing in continuity, and costs the slope unless the two slopes agree","w":["a joint always has a slope; it costs continuity","a joint never has a slope; it costs nothing","a joint has the slope of the left piece; it costs the right piece"],"why":"The slope from each side is that piece's slope. Equal heights make it continuous; equal slopes are a second, separate condition."},
 {"u":"13f4.1","q":"11f glued mx + 1 to x² − b at 2 with 2m + 1 = 4 − b. What second condition pins down one pair, and how do the pieces then meet?","a":"equal slopes at the joint: m = 2 · 2 = 4, and then b = −5; the pieces meet as a piece meets its tangent line","w":["equal second slopes: m = 2; the pieces cross","equal heights again: m = 1 and b = 1; the pieces meet at a corner","equal values at 0: b = −1; the pieces never meet"],"why":"The left slope is m and the right slope is 2x at 2. With the heights equal too, the line is the parabola's tangent line there."},
 {"u":"13f5.1","q":"What is the one thing this footing hands on about continuity and slopes?","a":"a slope means continuity, and never the other way: a function can be continuous at every real and have a slope at none","w":["a continuous function has a slope at most of its inputs","a function with no slope anywhere cannot be continuous","joints are the only places a slope can fail"],"why":"The 1872 function ended the belief that continuity brings slopes at most inputs. Slope-means-no-gap is all that survives of it."},
 {"u":"13f6.1","q":"What do reciprocal and quotient for slopes say, what are they built from, and why does the order in the quotient's top matter?","a":"(1/g)′ = −g′/g² and (f/g)′ = (f′g − fg′)/g², where g(w) ≠ 0; the first is a chain with 1/r, the second the product f · (1/g); the top is a difference, so swapping its terms flips the sign","w":["(1/g)′ = 1/g′ and (f/g)′ = f′/g′; built from the definition alone; the order does not matter","(1/g)′ = g′/g and (f/g)′ = (f′g + fg′)/g; built from slopes-add; the top is a sum","(1/g)′ = −1/g² and (f/g)′ = f′g − fg′; built from the power rule; the bottom may be 0"],"why":"The minus comes from 1/r's slope −1/r². Away-from-zero keeps g off 0 on an interval around w, so the hypothesis g(w) ≠ 0 is not decoration."},
 {"u":"13f7.1","q":"Why is “f′(w) = ½(f₁′(w) + f₂′(w)) at a joint” a move to watch out for?","a":"when the pieces arrive with different slopes there is no slope at all: the chord slopes from the two sides head to different reals, and their average is not a limit of anything","w":["the average should be weighted by the lengths of the pieces","the slope at a joint is the larger of the two","it is right whenever the joint is at 0"],"why":"Two-side-slopes asks the two to agree. |x| at 0 would get slope 0 by averaging, and it has none."},
 {"u":"13f8.1","q":"Handed a function in pieces, how does the work open and close? And handed a quotient-rule answer?","a":"find each joint by glued-functions, then compare the pieces' slopes there by joint-slopes: equal, a slope; unequal, none. For a quotient answer (f′g − fg′)/g², read g from the squared bottom and f from the shape of the top","w":["take the slope of each piece and add; read f from the bottom","check only the heights at the joints; multiply top and bottom","average the pieces; divide the top by the bottom"],"why":"To tune a family of pieces, write the two conditions, equal heights and equal slopes, and solve them together."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13g1.1","q":"On this step, what do “square”, “growing” and “cells” mean, and what is the cube of x?","a":"the square is the product x · x and nothing else; growing says only that the input changes, by an h of either sign; a cell is a box of 5i's grid, holding one product; the cube is x · x · x","w":["the square is a shape with four sides; growing means getting larger; cells are small squares; the cube is a solid","the square is 2x; growing means adding 1; cells are terms of a sum; the cube is 3x","the square is x + x; growing is a limit; cells are inputs; the cube is x² + x"],"why":"None of the everyday shapes is meant. The shapes that share the names wait for geometry on rung 18."},
 {"u":"13g2.1","q":"In the grid for (x + h)(x + h), which cells make the change Δy, and what becomes of each after dividing by h?","a":"x · h, h · x and h · h; divided by h they are x, x and h, so the chord slope is exactly 2x + h for every h ≠ 0","w":["only h · h; divided by h it is h","x · x and h · h; divided by h they are x and 1","all four cells; divided by h they give 2x"],"why":"The cell x · x is the old value and leaves in the subtraction. Nothing was set aside: the chord slope is 2x + h, not “about 2x”."},
 {"u":"13g3.1","q":"What is the h-count of a cell, what does count-of-h say, and why is “small” not the reason the h · h cell leaves?","a":"the number of factors h in it; count 0 leaves in the subtraction, count 1 divided by h stays in the limit, count 2 or more divided by h still holds an h and heads to 0; for one fixed h, h · h can be larger than 2xh","w":["the size of the cell; larger cells stay; h · h is always the smallest","the number of factors x; cells with no x leave; small cells are rounded off","the place of the cell in the grid; corner cells leave; h · h is negative"],"why":"Take x = 0.01 and h = 1. Size for one h decides nothing; the difference shows only after the division, in the limit."},
 {"u":"13g4.1","q":"How many cells of each h-count does (x + h)³ have, and how does counting give the slope of xⁿ?","a":"one of count 0, three of count 1, three of count 2 and one of count 3; (x + h)ⁿ has n cells of count 1, each xⁿ⁻¹ · h, so the slope is n · xⁿ⁻¹","w":["two of each count; xⁿ has two cells of count 1, so the slope is 2xⁿ⁻¹","one of count 0 and seven of count 1; the slope is 7x²","three of count 0 and five of count 3; the slope is 3x³"],"why":"The h of a count-1 cell can come from any one of the n factors. The second entry of every row of 4k's table is the power itself."},
 {"u":"13g5.1","q":"What does polynomial-chord say, and what second road to a polynomial's slope does it give?","a":"for a polynomial p and a real w, the chord slope (p(w + h) − p(w))/h equals a polynomial Q(h) for every h ≠ 0, and p′(w) = Q(0); so expand p(w + h), collect by h-count, and read off the coefficient of h","w":["the chord slope is constant in h; so p′(w) = p(w)","the chord slope has no limit; so polynomials need the product rule","Q(h) = p(h)/h; so p′(w) is the constant term of p"],"why":"A polynomial in h is continuous, so its limit at 0 is its value there. The road uses only 9g's algebra and one limit."},
 {"u":"13g6.1","q":"Why is “put h = 0, to remove the h · h cell” a move to watch out for?","a":"with h = 0 every cell holding an h is 0, the ones that should stay included, and the division by h is forbidden; the h · h cell leaves in the limit, after the division, with h never 0","w":["h = 0 removes only the cells of h-count 1","h = 0 makes the h · h cell negative","it is sound once the old value has been subtracted"],"why":"At h = 0 there is no change and no chord. The h-count, not a substitution, says which cells stay."},
 {"u":"13g7.1","q":"Handed the change Δy of some y, for every x and h, how does the work open, and how does it close?","a":"put x = 0 and call the change x: that gives y(x) − y(0), so y is fixed once one value is known; then run forward and check that the change comes out as given","w":["divide the change by h and stop","put h = 0 and read off y","take the slope of the change"],"why":"From Δy = 6xh + 3h², y(x) = 3x² + y(0). Backwards the change rebuilds the function, and it is exact."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13h1.1","q":"What does the power rule say in its usual form, which case is not yet licensed here, and what is the plan for it?","a":"(xⁿ)′ = n · xⁿ⁻¹: bring the power down in front and take one off the power; fraction powers are not yet licensed, and the plan is the slope of a q-th root, then a chain with the outside r ↦ r^p","w":["(xⁿ)′ = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1); whole powers are not licensed; the plan is induction","(xⁿ)′ = n · xⁿ; negative powers are not licensed; the plan is to avoid them","(xⁿ)′ = (n − 1)xⁿ; the power 0 is not licensed; the plan is a limit at 0"],"why":"Whole powers come from the product rule by induction, the power 0 from a constant, negative powers from the reciprocal. Each case has its own licence."},
 {"u":"13h2.1","q":"What does difference-of-powers say, what slope does root-slope give for x ↦ q√x at w > 0, and which limit does its proof lean on?","a":"u^q − v^q = (u − v)(u^(q−1) + u^(q−2)v + ⋯ + v^(q−1)); the slope is (1/q) · w^(1/q − 1); the proof leans on a q-th root landing where it is headed","w":["u^q − v^q = (u − v)^q; the slope is q · w^(q−1); the proof leans on the limit of 1/x","u^q − v^q = (u − v)(u + v); the slope is 1/(qw); the proof leans on no limit","u^q + v^q = (u + v)^q; the slope is w^(1/q); the proof leans on a sum of roots"],"why":"The long partner does for a q-th root what the conjugate did for √. With u and v the roots at w + h and w, the top becomes h."},
 {"u":"13h3.1","q":"What does fraction-power for slopes say, and which chain proves it?","a":"at every w > 0, x ↦ x^(p/q) has slope (p/q) · w^(p/q − 1); the chain has inside r = q√x and outside r^p","w":["x^(p/q) has slope (q/p) · w^(q/p); the chain has inside x^p and outside 1/r","x^(p/q) has slope p · w^(q − 1) at every real; no chain is needed","x^(p/q) has a slope only when p/q is a whole number; the chain has inside p/q"],"why":"Root-slope gives the inside's rate and the whole-power rule the outside's. Multiplied out, the product has the power rule's shape."},
 {"u":"13h4.1","q":"On which shape alone does the power rule fire, what is therefore always the first move, and why is “the slope of 1/x³ is 1/(3x²)” no rule?","a":"on c · xⁿ; so first rewrite roots and fractions as powers; 1/x³ is x⁻³, with slope −3x⁻⁴, while taking the slope of the bottom alone is licensed by nothing","w":["on any formula holding x; so first put in a number; 1/(3x²) is the right slope","on sums only; so first split the expression; 1/x³ has no slope","on xⁿ with n ≥ 1 only; so first clear fractions; the slope is 3x²"],"why":"At 1 the true slope is −3, not ⅓. Roots and fractions hide the power until it is written out."},
 {"u":"13h5.1","q":"What do constant-factor for slopes and lifting each say, and why does 2ˣ not fall under the power rule?","a":"a constant factor rides along, (cf)′ = cf′; an added constant leaves, (f + k)′ = f′; in 2ˣ the input is the power and the constant is the base, the other way round from c · xⁿ","w":["a constant factor leaves and an added constant rides; 2ˣ has slope x · 2ˣ⁻¹","both kinds of constant leave; 2ˣ is a constant","both kinds ride along; 2ˣ is a polynomial"],"why":"c multiplies every chord slope, and k cancels in every subtraction. The slope of 2ˣ waits for rung 17."},
 {"u":"13h6.1","q":"What does slopes-add license, what does it not, and why must the slope be taken before the input is put in?","a":"going term by term through a sum or difference, and nothing else: not through a product, a quotient or a chain; with the input put in first, what is left is a constant, whose slope is 0","w":["going factor by factor through a product; the input may go in at any time","term by term through any expression; the input must go in first","nothing for polynomials; the input changes the power"],"why":"Term by term works for sums and only sums. A slope is a fact about the function near the input, not about one value."},
 {"u":"13h7.1","q":"Why may a letter stand where a numeral stood in the power rule? Which input does the slope 2tx + b pick out, and how did 8i reach it?","a":"the rules ask only that the constant does not change when x does; 2tx + b = 0 at x = −b/(2t), the turning input of tx² + bx + c, which 8i reached by completing the square","w":["letters are always inputs; the slope picks out x = 0; 8i guessed it","a letter is a hidden numeral 1; the slope picks out x = b; 8i used the quadratic formula's two answers","letters have slope 1; the slope picks out x = c; 8i used a table"],"why":"One column then works for a whole family at once. Two roads, the square completed and the slope set to 0, reach the same input."},
 {"u":"13h8.1","q":"Why is “((3x)²)′ = 2 · 3x” a move to watch out for?","a":"(3x)² is 9x², with slope 18x: the rule fires on c · xⁿ, and here the 3 is inside the square, so the inside's rate 3 is missing","w":["the slope of a square is always 2","(3x)² is 3x², with slope 6x, so the move is right","a bracket has slope 0"],"why":"Rewrite first, or use the chain: 2 · (3x) · 3. At x = 1 the slope is 18, not 6."},
 {"u":"13h9.1","q":"How is the power rule run backwards, and which one slope has no answer here?","a":"go up one power and divide by the new power: x^m comes from x^(m+1)/(m + 1); the power −1 has no answer, since the new power would be 0 and cannot divide","w":["go down one power and multiply by the old one; the power 0 has no answer","divide by x; the power 1 has no answer","square the slope; negative powers have no answer"],"why":"Check by running forward with fraction-power and constant-factor. What has slope 1/x is left open on purpose."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13i1.1","q":"What does product for slopes say, which cells does each term come from, and why is (uv)′ = u′v′ no rule?","a":"(uv)′ = u′v + uv′; u′v comes from the cell Δu · v and uv′ from u · Δv, each divided by h; u′v′ would keep only the leftover cell Δu · Δv, the one that vanishes","w":["(uv)′ = u′v′; both terms come from the leftover cell; nothing is wrong with it","(uv)′ = u′ + v′; the terms come from the old product; u′v′ counts it twice","(uv)′ = uv; the cells are not used; u′v′ is its square"],"why":"Say the gesture as you write: this one, times that one's slope. For 3x the tempting rule gives 0 · 1 = 0, not 3."},
 {"u":"13i2.1","q":"What does three-factors say, and where do its three terms come from?","a":"(uvw)′ = u′vw + uv′w + uvw′; group as u · (vw) and use product for slopes twice, once outside and once on vw","w":["(uvw)′ = u′v′w′; from multiplying the three slopes","(uvw)′ = u′ + v′ + w′; from slopes-add","(uvw)′ = 3uvw; from the power rule"],"why":"Each term has one factor's slope beside the other two plain. Regrouping a product changes nothing, input by input."},
 {"u":"13i3.1","q":"What does quotient for slopes say in words, why does the order matter, and what does quotient-slope-sign say?","a":"bottom times the slope of the top, less top times the slope of the bottom, over the square of the bottom; taking away cannot be turned about; and since v² is positive, the slope has the sign of the top u′v − uv′","w":["top times bottom's slope, plus bottom times top's slope, over the bottom; order is free; the slope has the sign of v","slope of the top over slope of the bottom; order is free; the slope is always positive","bottom squared over the top; the order matters for adding; the slope has the sign of u"],"why":"A reversed order flips the sign: check on 1/x, whose slope is −1/x². The square in the bottom can never change a sign."},
 {"u":"13i4.1","q":"Why can the three roads to the slope of x/(x² + 1) not disagree, and what does the third road, multiplying up, never do?","a":"each was proved from the one definition, so each is a check on the others; multiplying up and taking slopes of both sides recomputes a slope known to exist, and never proves that one exists","w":["they are the same road written three ways; the third never uses the product rule","they agree only at whole inputs; the third never gives a formula","they can disagree, and the shortest is taken; the third never divides"],"why":"Product for slopes asks for y to have a slope before it is used. Here the quotient rule had already proved it."},
 {"u":"13i5.1","q":"Before reaching for the quotient rule, what do you ask, and when is splitting a fraction a move to watch out for?","a":"whether the fraction can be made a sum: a bottom that is a single power of x splits term by term; when the bottom is a sum it does not split, since the reciprocal of a sum is not the sum of the reciprocals","w":["whether the top is longer than the bottom; splitting is never sound","whether the bottom is 0; splitting is wrong when the top is a sum","whether the slope is positive; splitting is wrong for powers of x"],"why":"(x³ − 5x + 2)/x² = x − 5x⁻¹ + 2x⁻². The roads always agree, and picking the short one is the craft."},
 {"u":"13i6.1","q":"Why is (u/v)′ = (u′v − uv′)/v a move to watch out for?","a":"the bottom must be squared: one v comes from each reciprocal slope −v′/v², and for 1/x the unsquared form gives −1/x, not −1/x²","w":["the top should be a sum","the bottom should be v′","it is right whenever u is a constant"],"why":"A check on a known slope catches it at once. The square is also what makes quotient-slope-sign work."},
 {"u":"13i7.1","q":"What is the signature of a product-rule answer and of a quotient answer, and how far does running backwards find the function?","a":"a product answer has two terms, each factor plain in one and a slope in the other; a quotient answer has a squared bottom and a minus sign in the top; running backwards finds the function only up to a lift","w":["a product answer is one term; a quotient answer has no bottom; the function is found exactly","both have three terms; the function is found up to a factor","a product answer has a minus sign; a quotient answer a plus sign; nothing is found"],"why":"(2x − 1)/x and −1/x have the same slope, since they differ by the constant 2. No slope can see a lift."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13j1.1","q":"What is the dodge, and which letter does this ladder use for it?","a":"when an expression is too tangled to take directly, write a single letter for the tangled inside, so the outside becomes something managed at once; the ladder writes r","w":["drop the tangled part as small; the ladder writes h","expand every bracket first; the ladder writes x","guess the slope and check it; the ladder writes k"],"why":"Two slopes, one product, and then the letter goes back to what it stood for. The gesture matters, not the letter."},
 {"u":"13j2.1","q":"How do you find where to cut a chain, and why is calling x²(x + 1) the inside of x²(x + 1)³ wrong?","a":"work out a value by hand: the last thing done is the outside, and what it was done to is the inside; in x²(x + 1)³ the last thing done is the multiplying, so it is a product, not a chain with that inside","w":["cut at the first bracket; x²(x + 1) is the inside because it is in front","cut at the highest power; the inside is x²","cut anywhere; every choice gives the same slope"],"why":"The question is not “where is a bracket?” but “what was done last?” The cube there acts on x + 1 alone."},
 {"u":"13j3.1","q":"What are the four moves of the chain rule by hand, and why does the last matter as much as the others?","a":"name the inside r; take the outside's slope as if r were the input; take the inside's slope; multiply, and put back what r stood for; the answer must not hold a letter the question did not have","w":["expand, collect, take slopes, add; the last makes the answer shorter","name the outside, square it, divide by r, stop; the last is optional","put x = r, take one slope, add 1, check; the last is only for roots"],"why":"r was your name for the inside, and the question asked about x. For a hundredth power or a root there is no expanding to check by."},
 {"u":"13j4.1","q":"Why do rates through a chain multiply and not add, and where is the outside's rate taken?","a":"the outside is driven by the inside, as a gear by the gear before it, so each change is scaled twice; the outside's rate is taken where the inside has got to, at r₀, not at x","w":["rates add, since the two changes happen one after the other; the rate is taken at x","they multiply because both are positive; the rate is taken at 0","they multiply only for lines; the rate is taken at h"],"why":"For (x² + 1)³ at 1 the inside stands at 2: the outside's rate is 3 · 2² = 12, times the inside's 2, giving 24."},
 {"u":"13j5.1","q":"What does multiple-inside say, and what do a displacement inside and a multiple inside each do to a slope?","a":"for g(x) = f(kx), g′(w) = k · f′(kw); a displacement x − c has rate 1 and only moves where the slope is taken; a multiple kx moves where it is taken and multiplies it by k","w":["g′(w) = f′(w)/k; a displacement multiplies the slope by c; a multiple changes nothing","g′(w) = f′(kw); both leave the slope as it was","g′(w) = k + f′(w); a displacement adds c; a multiple adds k"],"why":"It is chain for slopes with the inside kx, whose slope is k. Run a clock k times as fast and every speed is multiplied by k."},
 {"u":"13j6.1","q":"How are a root of a bracket and one over a bracket taken by the chain, and what check with chords does the footing offer?","a":"write them as powers of r, r^(1/2) or r⁻², take the outside's slope at r and multiply by the inside's; for √(2x + 1) at 4 the slope ⅓ lies between the slopes of the chords on either side","w":["take the slope of the bracket and then its root; the chords have the same slope as the tangent","expand the root as a polynomial; the chords are not used","use the quotient rule on both; the chord from 0 gives the slope exactly"],"why":"√(2x + 1) has slope ½r^(−1/2) · 2 = 1/√(2x + 1). The chord check is a check here; why it must hold is rung 14's question."},
 {"u":"13j7.1","q":"What does chains-of-three say, how does it follow from chain for slopes, and which move is the one to watch?","a":"three rates through a chain of three multiply, each taken where its own input stands; use chain for slopes twice, peeling from the outside in; the move to watch is skipping the middle link's rate","w":["the three rates add; it follows from slopes-add; watch for a negative rate","only the outer and inner rates count; it follows from the product rule; watch the constant","the rates are averaged; it follows from the definition; watch for h = 0"],"why":"For ((2x + 1)² + 1)³ at 0: 12 · 2 · 2 = 48, not 12 · 2. Count the layers first: one rate per layer."},
 {"u":"13j8.1","q":"When a chain sits inside a product, which rule comes first, and where does the chain appear?","a":"the product rule comes first, since multiplying was the last thing done; the chain appears inside it, as the slope of the factor that is a chain","w":["the chain rule first, on the whole product","the power rule first, on the highest power","either first; the chain is not needed"],"why":"For x²(x + 1)³: 2x(x + 1)³ + x² · 3(x + 1)² · 1. At 1 that is 28, and expanding agrees."},
 {"u":"13j9.1","q":"Why is 3(x² + 1)² a wrong slope for (x² + 1)³, and where alone is it right by accident?","a":"it forgot the inside's rate 2x; it agrees with the true slope 6x(x² + 1)² only where the inside's slope is 1, at x = ½","w":["it forgot to lower the power; it is right at x = 0","it took the outside's slope at x; it is right at x = 1","it is the true slope; it is right everywhere"],"why":"A wrong rule can meet the truth at one input. Test a slope at an input where the inside's rate is not 1."},
 {"u":"13j10.1","q":"How is a chain-rule answer read backwards, and when can a missing factor be supplied?","a":"as an outside's rate, written in the inside, times the inside's rate: 5(3x − 1)⁴ · 3 came from (3x − 1)⁵; a missing constant factor can be supplied, since constants ride, and a missing factor holding x cannot","w":["as a product of two unrelated factors; any missing factor can be supplied","as a sum of two rates; no factor can ever be supplied","as the outside alone; a missing x can be supplied and a constant cannot"],"why":"Where the inside's slope is off by more than a constant, expand and go term by term. Two backward readings can differ by a lift, which no slope sees."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13k1.1","q":"What does y − y₀ = m(x − x₀) say in words, and why is the multiplied form kept rather than the fraction form?","a":"how far up from the point equals m times how far across from the point; the fraction form has a hole at the point itself, and the multiplied form mends it","w":["the output equals m times the input plus the start b; the fraction form is harder to write","how far across equals m times how far up; the fraction form is wrong at every input","the chord slope is m at one input only; the multiplied form loses the point itself"],"why":"At x = x₀ the multiplied form reads 0 = m · 0, so the point passes. The gain of answers where x − x₀ is 0 is wanted here, and the start b is never needed."},
 {"u":"13k2.1","q":"What are the three moves for a tangent line, in order, and why must x₀ go into the slope before the line is written?","a":"read the point, take the slope at the point, write the line; the slope must be a number, since x left in it makes a line of degree 2, not a linear function","w":["take the slope function, write the line, then put x₀ in; the order does not matter","write the line, read the point, take the slope; x₀ goes in last to keep the formula general","read the point, write the line, take the slope; x₀ is never put into the slope"],"why":"For x² at 3: the point is (3, 9), the slope is 2 · 3 = 6, the line is y − 9 = 6(x − 3). Writing 2x(x − 3) leaves x in the slope."},
 {"u":"13k3.1","q":"Which two facts does a tangent line carry about the function, and how do you find where a given line touches?","a":"the function's value at the point and its slope there; match the slope to find x₀, then check that the line and the function have the same value at x₀","w":["the function's value everywhere near the point; solve line = function and stop","the slope at every input; match the values and never check the slope","the function's zero and its start; put 0 in for x"],"why":"y = 4x − 4 and x²: 2x₀ = 4 gives x₀ = 2, and both give 4 there. Matching slope alone is not enough: y = 4x has slope 4 too, and does not touch."},
 {"u":"13k4.1","q":"What are L(x₀ + h) and E(h), and what does the nearby-line-error technique say?","a":"L is f(x₀) + f′(x₀) · h, the tangent line's value, and E is the true value less L; E(h)/h heads to 0, and the tangent line is the only line through the point whose error does that","w":["L is the chord's value and E is the chord slope; E(h) is 0 for small h","L is f(x₀ + h) and E is f′(x₀) · h; E(h) heads to 0 for every line through the point","L is the slope function and E is its error; E(h)/h heads to f′(x₀)"],"why":"E(h)/h is the chord slope less the slope, which heads to 0. Every line through the point has an error heading to 0; only the tangent line's shrinks faster than the step."},
 {"u":"13k5.1","q":"How is an estimate by hand made, and what does the root's-nearby-line technique say?","a":"pick a base x₀ where the value and the slope are known and let h be the small awkward part; for √ the linearization always gives more than the true root","w":["pick any base and a large h; for √ the linearization always gives less than the true root","pick h = 0 and read the function; for √ the linearization is exact","pick a base where the slope is 0; for √ the linearization is too big for h > 0 and too small for h < 0"],"why":"√4.1 from 4: 2 + ¼ · 0.1 = 2.025, and 2.025² = 4.100625, just over 4.1. The square of the line's value is x₀ + h plus a square, so it is over whichever way h goes."},
 {"u":"13k6.1","q":"How does the error change when the step is cut by ten, what decides which way it goes, and what happens when the step is too far?","a":"it drops by about a hundred; the way the graph bends away from its tangent line decides the side; too far a step gives a poor estimate, so choose a nearer base","w":["it drops by ten; the sign of the slope decides the side; a far step only costs time","it stays the same; the sign of h decides the side; a far step is still exact","it drops by a thousand; the value at the base decides the side; a far step turns the error about"],"why":"For x² the error is h² exactly. From 4 the line says √8 is about 3, but 3² = 9; from 9 it gives 17/6 ≈ 2.8333 against 2.8284."},
 {"u":"13k7.1","q":"What is the graph of the slope function, and what does the slope-sign-near technique say and not say?","a":"the pairs of each input with the tangent line's slope there; where the slope is positive, f is higher just to the right and lower just to the left; it does not say f climbs on a whole interval","w":["the tangent line at one input; where the slope is positive, f is positive; it does not say f has a slope","the graph of f lifted; where the slope is positive, f climbs on every interval; it does not say anything near one input","the pairs of each output with its input; where the slope is 0, f is 0; it does not say the tangent line is level"],"why":"For x² the slope function is 2x. Slope-sign-near is about one input and its near neighbours; climbing on a whole interval is rung 14's technique."},
 {"u":"13k8.1","q":"Why is “L(x₀ + h) is exact for small h” a move to watch out for?","a":"the line's error shrinks faster than the step, yet it is not 0: for x² it is h² exactly, however small h is","w":["the line's error grows as h shrinks","the line is exact for small h, and only wrong for large h","the line has no value at x₀ + h when h is small"],"why":"Small is not zero. Nearby-line-error says E(h)/h heads to 0; it never says E(h) is 0 for any h other than 0."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13l1.1","q":"What is a branch of a solution set through (x₀, y₀), and why is there no branch of y · y = x through (0, 0)?","a":"a function on an open interval around x₀, with value y₀ at x₀, whose every pair is in the solution set; no pair of y · y = x has x < 0, so no open interval around 0 can be its inputs","w":["the whole solution set, when it passes the column-test; (0, 0) is not in the solution set","any one pair of the solution set; at (0, 0) there are two pairs in the column","a function on a closed interval ending at x₀; at (0, 0) the slope is 0"],"why":"Near (4, 2) the branch is √x and near (4, −2) it is −√x. At (0, 0) every open interval around 0 holds negative inputs, where y · y = x has no pair."},
 {"u":"13l2.1","q":"What licenses taking the slope of both sides of an equation on a branch, and why does y bring a factor y′?","a":"on a branch the two sides are functions of x that agree on an open interval, so they have the same slope; y is the branch's output, the inside of a chain, and chain for slopes multiplies by y′","w":["any true equation may have the slope of both sides taken; y is a constant, so its slope is y′","the two sides agree at one input, which is enough; y′ is put in to balance the units","adding-keeps-answers; y is the input, so its slope is 1 and y′ is written for it"],"why":"Slopes-decided-near needs agreement on an open interval around x₀. For y · y = x that gives 2y · y′ = 1, so y′ = 1/(2y) where y ≠ 0."},
 {"u":"13l3.1","q":"Why is the solution set of x³ + y³ = 9 one branch everywhere, and why is the formula −x²/y² right to stop where y = 0?","a":"each x gives exactly one y, the cube root of 9 − x³, so the whole set is one function; where y = 0 that function has no slope, since the cube root has none at 0","w":["it passes through (0, 0); where y = 0 the slope is 0","each x gives three values of y; where y = 0 the three branches meet","it is a linear function; where y = 0 the formula gives the slope −x²"],"why":"Taking slopes of both sides gives 3x² + 3y² · y′ = 0. The formula finds a slope only where one exists; the stop at y = 0 marks where the branch has none."},
 {"u":"13l4.1","q":"Why is “x · x = 4, so 2x = 0” a move to watch out for?","a":"x · x = 4 is true at 2 and −2 only, not on an open interval, so slopes-decided-near gives no licence to take slopes of both sides","w":["the slope of x · x is x, so the line should read x = 0","the slope of 4 is 4, so the line should read 2x = 4","the line is right: 2x = 0 gives the answer x = 0"],"why":"Slopes of both sides need two functions that agree near the input. An equation true at two inputs only says nothing of slopes, and 2x = 0 would say x = 0."},
 {"u":"13l5.1","q":"What does it mean for f to keep order on A, and what does the inverse-is-continuous technique say?","a":"for inputs a < b in A, f(a) < f(b); if f is continuous and keeps order on an open interval, its outputs include an open interval around y₀, and the function running it backwards is continuous at y₀","w":["f(a) = f(b) for all a, b in A; then f has a slope at every input","for a < b in A, f(a) > f(b); then the function running it backwards has slope 0","f sends A to A; then f is its own undoing"],"why":"13e's undoing's-slope assumed the undoing had a slope. This is the first half of the answer to when it has one: the undoing is continuous."},
 {"u":"13l6.1","q":"What does the inverse-slope technique say, which hypothesis of 13e's undoing's-slope does it remove, and why is f′(x₀) ≠ 0 not decoration?","a":"g, running f backwards, has a slope at y₀ = f(x₀), and it is 1/f′(x₀); it no longer assumes g has a slope; x³ has slope 0 at 0, and its undoing, the cube root, has no slope there","w":["g′(y₀) = −f′(x₀); it no longer assumes f is continuous; with f′(x₀) = 0 the slope of g is 0","g′(y₀) = f′(x₀); it no longer assumes f has a slope; with f′(x₀) = 0 nothing changes","g′(x₀) = 1/f′(x₀); it no longer assumes f keeps order; with f′(x₀) = 0 the slope of g is 1"],"why":"The slopes are taken at matching inputs and multiply to 1. Nothing times 0 is 1, so a level tangent line leaves the undoing without a slope."},
 {"u":"13l7.1","q":"What does the inverse dodge give for the q-th root, and for the inverse of x³ + x at 2?","a":"for the q-th root at y₀, 1 over q · x₀^(q−1) with x₀ the root of y₀; for the inverse of x³ + x at 2, the matching input is 1 and the slope there is 4, so ¼","w":["for the q-th root, q · x₀^(q−1); for the inverse at 2, 1/13","for the q-th root, 1/q; for the inverse at 2, 4","for the q-th root, x₀^q; for the inverse at 2, ½"],"why":"1³ + 1 = 2, and the slope 3x² + 1 is 4 at 1. The dodge gives the slope of a function the ladder cannot write as a formula."},
 {"u":"13l8.1","q":"Why is the slope of the inverse at 2 not 1/f′(2)?","a":"the slopes that multiply to 1 are taken at matching inputs: the pair (1, 2) on f goes to (2, 1) on the inverse, so the reciprocal is of f's slope at 1, not at 2","w":["the reciprocal must be taken of f(2), not of f′(2)","the inverse has no slope at 2","1/f′(2) is right; the care is only in the sign"],"why":"For x³ + x, f′(2) = 13 is the slope at the wrong input. The inverse's input 2 is f's output; f's input there is 1, where the slope is 4."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13m"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13m"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13m1.1","q":"What is the tangent step N(x), how is it read in words, and when is it licensed?","a":"N(x) = x − f(x)/f′(x): the new guess is the old guess less the value over the slope; it is licensed where f has a slope other than 0","w":["N(x) = x − f′(x)/f(x): the old guess less the slope over the value; licensed where f(x) ≠ 0","N(x) = x + f(x) · f′(x): the old guess plus the value times the slope; licensed everywhere","N(x) = f(x)/f′(x): the value over the slope; licensed where f is a polynomial"],"why":"N(x) is where the tangent line at x is 0. A linear function with a slope other than 0 is 0 at exactly one input; a level line is never 0."},
 {"u":"13m2.1","q":"What is the averaging step for √2, why does it close in, and how do its correct places compare with bisection's?","a":"N(x) = ½(x + 2/x); if x is too large then 2/x is too small, so √2 lies between them; its correct places about double each step, where bisection adds about a third of a place","w":["N(x) = ½(x + 2); x and 2 trap √2; it adds one place a step, as bisection does","N(x) = x − 2/x; the product is 2; its places halve each step","N(x) = 2/x; it swaps too large for too small; bisection is faster"],"why":"From 1: 3/2, 17/12, 577/408, 665857/470832, with 1, 3, 6, 12 correct places. Twelve places by bisection from [1, 2] take 40 halvings."},
 {"u":"13m3.1","q":"What does the polynomial-guess-error technique say, and what does places-double add to it?","a":"for a polynomial, each new error is the old error squared, times a factor; places-double adds that once a guess is close enough, each new guess about doubles the number of correct places","w":["each new error is half the old error; places-double adds one place a step","each new error is the old error times the slope; places-double says the error is 0 after two steps","each new error is the old error plus a factor; places-double holds from any start"],"why":"For √2, N(x) − √2 = (x − √2)²/(2x). An error near 0.001 squared is near 0.000001: three places become six."},
 {"u":"13m4.1","q":"What is the tangent step for ∛2, and why does the start matter?","a":"N(x) = (2x + 2/x²)/3, a weighted halfway real of x, x and 2/x²; a near start is already in the place-doubling stretch, where a far one wastes steps getting close","w":["N(x) = ½(x + 2/x²); every start gives the same guesses","N(x) = (x + 2/x)/3; the start decides which zero is found","N(x) = x − 3x²; a far start is faster"],"why":"From 1 the guesses run 4/3, 91/72, 1.25993⋯. Starting from 1 wastes a step; 5/4 is already close. Choosing the start is part of the skill."},
 {"u":"13m5.1","q":"How did the oldest working find a zero of y³ − 2y − 5 without slopes, and what did it keep and set aside?","a":"it put in a guess plus a small unknown, 2 + p, and set aside every piece with p² or more; it kept 10p − 1 = 0, so p = 0.1: the linearization, with the error set aside","w":["it bisected between 2 and 3; it kept the trap and set aside the midpoint","it kept p³ + 6p² and set aside 10p − 1; the slope pieces were dropped","it guessed 2 and stopped; nothing was set aside"],"why":"(2 + p)³ − 2(2 + p) − 5 = p³ + 6p² + 10p − 1. The kept piece is the value −1 plus the slope 10 times the step: 13k's nearby line."},
 {"u":"13m6.1","q":"In which ways can the tangent line mislead the step, and what is the working rule?","a":"a level or nearly level line names nothing or lands far away, the guesses can cycle for ever, or run further off each step; the rule is trap first, then sharpen","w":["only by rounding; the rule is to carry more places","only at a zero itself; the rule is to start far from the zero","never, once f has a slope; the rule is to step until the guesses repeat"],"why":"x² − 2 from 0.1 lands at 10.05; x³ − 2x + 2 from 0 runs 0, 1, 0, 1; the cube root gives N(x) = −2x. Bisect until the trap is small and the slope inside is far from 0."},
 {"u":"13m7.1","q":"How does an error in a measured input spread to the output, and what is the relative error?","a":"the output changes by about the slope times the input's error; the relative error is the error as a part of the whole, Δx/x","w":["the output changes by the input's error squared; the relative error is Δx · x","the output changes by the same amount as the input; the relative error is x/Δx","the output does not change; the relative error is the error less the value"],"why":"x = 4.9t² at 2.0 s with 0.1 s either way: 19.6 · 0.1 = 1.96 m. So 5% in the time became 10% in the distance."},
 {"u":"13m8.1","q":"What does the relative-errors technique say, and what happens to relative errors through a product?","a":"through a power the relative error is multiplied by the power, and the constant factor does not matter; through a product the relative errors of the factors add","w":["through a power the relative error is raised to the power; through a product they multiply","through a power the relative error is unchanged; through a product the larger one wins","through a power the error is multiplied by the constant; through a product they cancel"],"why":"A square doubles it, a cube triples it, a square root halves it. Product for slopes gives (uv)′/(uv) = u′/u + v′/v."},
 {"u":"13m9.1","q":"Running it backwards, what is the experimenter's question, and how is it answered?","a":"how good must my measurement be? Divide the error you can bear in the output by the power","w":["how large is my output? Multiply the input's error by the power","which power fits my data? Divide the output by the input","how many measurements do I need? Square the error you can bear"],"why":"For B = 70M^(3/4) known to within 3%, the mass must be known to within 4%. For the fall's distance to 1%, the time to ½%."},
 {"u":"13m10.1","q":"Why is xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f′(xₙ)/f(xₙ) a move to watch out for?","a":"it is upside down: the walk down the tangent line is value over slope, and for x² − 2 from 1 the wrong form gives 3, not 3/2","w":["it should add, not take away","it is right for polynomials and wrong only for roots","it leaves out the factor ½"],"why":"The right step is 1 − (−1)/2 = 3/2. The wrong one is 1 − 2/(−1) = 3, which moves away from √2."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["13n"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13n"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13n1.1","q":"What does the rate-units technique say, and why does the limit keep the units of the chord slopes?","a":"f′ has the units of the output over the units of the input, and f″ the output's over the input's squared; every chord slope on the way to the limit carries those units","w":["f′ has the units of the output; the limit drops the input's unit as h goes to 0","f′ has the units of the input over the output; the limit turns the units about","f′ has no units; a limit is a bare number"],"why":"Distance in metres at times in seconds: the speed is in metres per second, and its slope, the acceleration, in metres per second per second."},
 {"u":"13n2.1","q":"What does the unit-check technique say, and why can a line pass it and still be wrong?","a":"in a correct line every term of a sum and both sides of = have the same units; the check catches a missing factor with a unit, not a wrong number","w":["every term must have different units; a line that passes is right","only the two sides of = need the same units; a line that fails may still be right","units are checked after the numbers; a line that passes has no wrong numbers"],"why":"A line that fails is wrong; a line that passes may still be wrong. A wrong bare number, such as a dropped ½, carries no unit for the check to catch."},
 {"u":"13n3.1","q":"What does the conversion technique say, and how does the chain rule give the same answer for a rate?","a":"multiply by a fraction equal to 1 whose bottom cancels the old unit, top and bottom for a rate; changing the input's unit is an inside with a constant slope, and the chain multiplies the rate by it","w":["add the new unit's size to the number; the chain rule adds the same constant","divide by the new unit and drop the old; the chain rule does not apply to units","multiply the number by itself; the chain rule squares the rate"],"why":"90 km/h · (1000 m/1 km) · (1 h/3600 s) = 25 m/s. A rate per minute is 60 times the rate per second: in a minute the cart goes 60 times as far."},
 {"u":"13n4.1","q":"Why was a metal cylinder a weak standard for the kilogram, and what fixes the kilogram since 2019?","a":"a thing can change, and nothing could say whether it or its copies had moved, since the cylinder was the kilogram by definition; since 2019 an exact constant fixes it","w":["it was too heavy to copy; since 2019 a larger cylinder fixes it","it was lost in 1889; since 2019 the mass of a litre of water fixes it","it never changed, which made it hard to check; since 2019 nothing fixes it"],"why":"The official copies drifted from it by some tens of micrograms over a century. A number does not drift, and anyone with the right apparatus can realise the unit from it."},
 {"u":"13n5.1","q":"What fixed the kelvin before 2019, what fixes it now, and why is a rate per kelvin the same number as the rate per °C?","a":"the triple point of water, set at 273.16 K exactly; now the Boltzmann constant, exact; the two scales differ only by a lift, so their steps are the same size","w":["the boiling point of water; now the triple point; the two scales have steps of different sizes","absolute zero alone; now the freezing point of water; a rate per kelvin is 273.16 times the rate per °C","the Boltzmann constant; now the triple point of water; the rates differ by a lift"],"why":"The triple point of water is now a measured value. A lift changes no slope (13d's lifting), so a rate per kelvin and per °C agree."},
 {"u":"13n6.1","q":"What is fixed in the mole since 2019, what was fixed before, and how many atoms are in 6 g of carbon-12?","a":"the Avogadro constant, 6.022 140 76 × 10²³ per mole, exactly; before, the mole was as many pieces as atoms in 12 g of carbon-12; 6 g is half a mole, about 3.011 × 10²³ atoms","w":["the mass of a mole of carbon-12; before, the Avogadro constant; about 6.022 × 10²³ atoms","the mass of one atom; before, nothing; about 1.2 × 10²⁴ atoms","the Avogadro constant, by measurement; before, 16 g of oxygen; exactly 6 atoms"],"why":"The mole counts by weighing. Since 2019 the count is exact and the mass of a mole of carbon-12 is a measured value, very near 12 g."},
 {"u":"13n7.1","q":"What were the two scales before 1961, why did they differ, and why does a table give hydrogen as 1.008 and stop there?","a":"chemists set natural oxygen at 16 and physicists set oxygen-16 at 16; natural oxygen is a mix of isotopes; natural hydrogen is a mix too, and the mix varies from sample to sample","w":["chemists used carbon and physicists hydrogen; they measured in different units; the table has no room for more places","both set oxygen-16 at 16; they differed by rounding; hydrogen's mass is exactly 1.008","chemists set hydrogen at 1 and physicists at 2; hydrogen has no isotopes; more places are secret"],"why":"The scales differed by about 3 parts in 10 000. In 1959 to 1961 both sides agreed on carbon-12 at 12. More places of a varying mix would claim too much."},
 {"u":"13n8.1","q":"Why is “passes the unit check, so it is right” a move to watch out for?","a":"the check can only fail a line; a line with the right units may still carry a wrong number","w":["the check is only for sums, never for equalities","a line that passes the check has wrong units","the check must be run twice before it counts"],"why":"Unit-check: a line that fails is wrong; a line that passes may still be wrong. The check sees units, not bare numbers."},
 {"u":"13n9.1","q":"Handed f(x) in joules at x in kelvin, what are the units of f′ and of f″?","a":"f′ is in joules per kelvin and f″ in joules per kelvin squared","w":["f′ is in joules and f″ in kelvin","f′ is in kelvin per joule and f″ in kelvin squared per joule","f′ is in joule kelvins and f″ in joule kelvins squared"],"why":"Rate-units: the output's units over the input's, and for f″ over the input's unit squared."});
